16.4.content:
Any request by either party to modify the approved scope, deliverables, assumptions, phase baseline, timeline, integration inventory, delivery responsibilities, partner onboarding sequence, acceptance structure, or commercial impact of this SOW shall be governed through the formal Change Management Process described in Section 17. No requested change shall be treated as approved, committed, or included in delivery unless it has been documented, reviewed, and formally authorized in accordance with that process.

Change control applies, at a minimum, to any proposed modification involving:
- the addition of any new geography, site, warehouse, legal entity, foundry, OSAT partner, or trade lane beyond the approved baseline;
- the addition of any new interface, message type, source system, target system, data feed, downstream consumer, or partner-specific format not expressly included in the approved integration scope;
- any expansion of process, technical, application, services, testing, deployment, training, hypercare, or reporting scope beyond the approved phase baseline;
- any change to the approved solution architecture, system-of-record model, middleware responsibility model, or boundary between planning, execution, collaboration, genealogy, test-data, and analytics platforms;
- any change to the approved Kinaxis environment or instance strategy, infrastructure sizing assumptions, or related design dependencies;
- any change to the approved procurement, subcontracting, ordering, traceability, or planning design baseline that materially affects effort, sequence, interfaces, testing, or deployment;
- any request to include capabilities currently identified as deferred, future-phase, excluded, client-owned, or pending confirmation elsewhere in this SOW.

The parties acknowledge that this engagement includes material design dependencies and deferred decisions that must be resolved in a controlled manner before related build or deployment commitments are treated as in scope. These include, as applicable, `<CONFIRM: demand signaling ownership and planning-input ownership>`, `<CONFIRM: BOM explosion ownership and planning-process design>`, `<CONFIRM: target-state Kinaxis instance strategy>`, `<CONFIRM: final process ownership model across Google and partner teams>`, `<CONFIRM: final phase-by-phase rollout baseline and go-live content>`, and `<TECH TEAM: final system-of-record ownership, integration ownership, and technical design authority across the in-scope platforms>`. If resolution of any such item changes the approved baseline, the resulting impact shall be handled through Section 17 rather than absorbed informally into ongoing delivery.

No workstream, milestone, or release shall proceed on the assumption that an unresolved item has been approved by implication because design, build, testing, or planning activity has started. Any request, dependency, assumption deviation, or decision that may affect scope, effort, delivery dates, resource needs, acceptance criteria, or commercials shall be documented and routed through the governance structure for impact assessment and disposition under Section 17.

Pending formal approval under the Change Management Process in Section 17:
- Bristlecone shall have no obligation to perform work outside the approved SOW baseline;
- Google shall have no obligation to accept or pay for work that has not been formally approved as part of the agreed scope;
- indicative future-phase items, design options, and exploratory discussions shall not be interpreted as committed delivery obligations;
- excluded items and client-retained responsibilities shall remain excluded or client-owned unless expressly incorporated through an approved change.

Where a requested change affects milestone dates, phase sequencing, release readiness, deployment timing, acceptance timelines, staffing assumptions, or deliverable due dates, the integrated project plan and any impacted deliverables shall be updated only after the applicable change has been approved through Section 17. Where a requested change affects commercials, invoicing basis, rate application, travel requirements, or any other contractual or payment-related term governed by the underlying agreement, such changes shall also require `<LEGAL: confirm required amendment, addendum, or governing-agreement review for commercial or contractual changes>`.

If a dependency shortfall, client delay, third-party readiness issue, partner onboarding delay, access issue, environment issue, or data-readiness issue requires the parties to adopt an interim workaround or temporary manual process, that workaround shall be documented, time-bounded, and approved through the applicable governance forum. Any material impact arising from such workaround, including additional effort, schedule change, risk exposure, or change in delivery responsibility, shall be evaluated under the Change Management Process in Section 17.

For clarity, the change-control principles in this section apply equally to increases and reductions in scope, deferrals of approved functionality, resequencing of releases, substitution of delivery approach, and changes to responsibility boundaries between Google, Bristlecone, external partners, and Google-managed platform teams. All such changes shall be governed through the formal process in Section 17 so that scope, timelines, deliverables, acceptance expectations, and commercials remain controlled and mutually approved.
